model to annotate or classify regions of a genome","Method developers comparing a new DNA language model with published ones"],"decision":"Choose a DNA language model, and whether to probe its frozen embeddings or fine-tune it, for a sequence annotation task.","inputs":["DNA sequences of the element type of interest, with genome build and species","Labelled examples, or none if only a frozen probe is planned","A compute budget, which decides whether fine-tuning is possible"],"output":"Per-task scores for each model under each training regime, with the task's split, kept separate by benchmark.","setting":"Research genome annotation from sequence. Short-range and long-range tasks, frozen probes and fine-tuning are separate comparisons; a rank on one does not transfer to another.","exclusions":["Variant effect prediction, which has its own use cases for missense, regulatory, rare-disease and splicing variants","Sequence conservation scores and synthetic promoter expression level, which are not element annotation","Pooling scores across benchmarks: each runs its own models, splits and probes"],"clinical_scope":"Research only. Nothing recorded here supports a clinical or diagnostic decision.","evidence_gaps":["The linked evidence rests on five studies: GenBench, the GENEB leaderboard, SegmentNT, the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark and GUANinE v1.1. The models they cover overlap only partly, so a model ranked well in one may be absent from the others.","BEND, DART-Eval, GUE, Genomic Benchmarks and Gene-MTEB results are in the store on task records with no protocol, so they cannot carry a judgement yet.","GENEB is recorded as two benchmark records, the leaderboard and GENEB, which are probably one benchmark.","No conventional baseline, such as a k-mer or position-weight-matrix classifier, is recorded as the reference these models must beat.","Automated source review only. No human scientific review, and nothing here was reproduced."],"search_terms":["DNA language model","